// src/std/core/loop/loop_handle.zen
// the enclosing loop, as a value.
//
// LoopHandle is to a loop what @scope is to a block - DESIGN.md
// says so in as many words - and it is declared the same way
// Scope is: a struct of signatures with no bodies and no storage.
//
// THESE THREE ARE INTRINSICS. Nobody impls LoopHandle; the
// compiler provides it, the way it provides @Self. Pretending they
// are ordinary functions would be the lie, because no ordinary
// function can do what they do: `next` and `break` do not return
// to their caller, and there is no unwinding machinery in this
// language for a library to reach for.
//
// What they ARE is the general non-local exit, which is one
// mechanism and not two special cases:
//
//   > A non-escaping closure may return through its caller.
//   > `.try()` and `h.break()` are both this.
//
// `.try()` unwraps Ok or returns the Err from the enclosing
// FUNCTION; `h.break(v)` returns v from the enclosing LOOP. Same
// jump, same frame boundary, same reason it is legal - the body is
// a stack closure, so its caller's frame is still on the stack
// underneath it. An escaping closure may not, having no caller
// frame to return through. So `loop` stays a genuinely ordinary
// function: break is a language feature loop uses, not a privilege
// the compiler grants to one name.
//
// A LoopHandle is non-escaping for the same reason @scope is: it
// names a frame that is about to stop existing. It may be passed
// INWARD - that is exactly what `loop(range, body)` does when it
// hands `h` to the body - but never stored in a struct, returned,
// or captured by an escaping closure.

LoopHandle* = {
    // continue: leave THIS pass, run the next one. The rest of the
    // body is skipped; the loop is not.
    next* = (self: @Self) ()

    // break with nothing: the loop evaluates to None.
    break* = (self: @Self) ()

    // break with a value: the loop evaluates to Ok(value), which is
    // what makes a loop an expression. T is the enclosing loop's
    // own T - the one in its Res<T> - so it is fixed by the loop
    // this handle names, never by the call.
    //
    // The `()` return type is a formality: neither break returns to
    // its caller, so nothing ever observes it. `()` is the honest
    // spelling of "you get nothing back" in a language whose type
    // for "never returns" would be a fourth thing to explain.
    break*<T> = (self: @Self, value: T) ()
}
